with the us entering a recession and  inflation reaching multi-decade highs  there's no doubt that the western world  has been on edge for quite some time  many have been struggling to make ends  meet whether that be paying for gas or  keeping up with the skyrocketing rent  even those that make a moderate salary  have been worried as they've seen their  monthly savings go down substantially  but all of this is quite insignificant  to the economic situation in lebanon you  see lebanon has not only been facing an  economic cooldown but a full-on economic  crisis  their gdp growth rate has been negative  for years now and things have gotten  especially bad over the past two years  this has cost their gdp to tumble from  nearly 55 billion dollars to a low of 18  billion dollars to make things worse  lebanon is not just fighting a shrinking  economy but also hyperinflation if you  thought america's nine percent inflation  was terrible just look at this when the  pandemic started it lebanon's inflation  rate roared to 150 percent for a little  bit it seemed like things were cooling  down but then they saw another surge  that took inflation out all the way to  over 200  despite this horrendous situation the  western world at large hasn't really  paid much attention the story of lebanon  has been buried by fewer surrounding the  u.s stock market the fed inflation the  supply shortage home prices ukraine and  so on now i don't blame the west for  ignoring lebanon given how many internal  issues they've been dealing with  themselves but in the sake of telling  lebanon's story here's a lebanon  collapse right beneath our feet without  anyone even noticing  taking a look back it might seem like  lebanon's economic issue started  alongside the pandemic but that was just  the beginning of the collapse lebanon's  economic turmoil can actually be traced  all the way back to 1975.  this was the year that a group of  phalanges decided to attack a bus filled  with palestinian refugees traveling to  lebanon with the tensions already  running hot between these two groups it  didn't take long for this attack to  escalate into a conflict and eventually  a full-on civil war to make things worse  this wasn't just a two or five year war  it ended up lasting at 15 years i don't  think you'd be surprised here that  lebanon was devastated economically by  the time the war came to an end but  fortunately the rest of the world came  to their aid lebanon received tons of  foreign along with massive gifts from  neighboring countries which allowed them  to build up significant central bank  reserves meanwhile millions of lebanese  had found work abroad throughout the  civil war and many of them sent back  substantial amounts to their families  while these were all extremely kind to  gestures looking back it might have done  more harm than good because it prevented  lebanon's economy from truly developing  lebanon became dependent on foreign aid  while its citizens became dependent on  family members living abroad  lebanon further leaned into this  dependency by pegging their currency  with the u.s dollar the central bank  promised that they would always exchange  1507 lebanese liras for one us dollar  this wasn't a very difficult promise to  fulfill in the beginning given that  lebanon had plenty of foreign currencies  rolling in but over time this dried up  you see it citizens with family members  living abroad either passed away or  moved abroad themselves it also didn't  help that virtually everyone who had the  opportunity to leave the country did  exactly that between 1975 and 1990  nearly a million people left the country  and between 1990 and 2019 another 750  000 people left  aside from declining demographics  western countries also started pulling  back substantially on their aid you see  it when the west started supporting  lebanon in the early 1990s the middle  east wasn't really seeing it as an enemy  of course there was the persian gulf war  but the main enemy in most westerners  minds was the russian commies with the  collapse of the soviet union in 1991  however the communist threat slowly  subsided and we saw middle eastern fears  grow and this of course climaxed with 9  11 with middle eastern tension reaching  all-time highs it's not surprising that  western politicians did not want to  continue providing aid to countries like  lebanon it simply wasn't a good look for  their administration to make things  worse a terrorist group in lebanon named  hezbollah were starting to gain some  traction and this basically cut off all  foreign aid banks struggled to maintain  the conversion rate between liras and  dollars and it wasn't long until they  resorted to fraudulent practices  to maintain a pegged conversion rate you  need a strong supply of both currencies  lebanese banks had plenty of liras but  virtually no dollars  now they could have made a lira as a  free-floating currency meaning that its  value would fluctuate based on supply  and demand but given the weak  fundamentals of lebanon the central bank  knew that liras would get destroyed by  the free markets so they decided to keep  the peg and pop up the lira for as long  as possible to accomplish this they  needed more dollars and what better way  to attract dollars than to pump up  interest rates  lebanon would pump up interest rates at  15 and eventually even 20 for  individuals who deposited dollars but as  you would guess such rates were  impossible to achieve through legitimate  means more times than not lebanon was  simply borrowing money to pay these high  interest rates and keep the country  running which led to the national debt  getting out of hand between 2012 and  2019 lebanon's debt to gdp ratio would  rocket from 131 percent which is already  high to 175  for perspective only four countries in  the world have a higher debt to gdp  ratio and the three of these countries  have already collapsed economically so  it was just a matter of time until  lebanon followed in their footsteps but  nonetheless the lebanese government  refused to give in with such a high debt  to gdp ratio it became extremely  difficult to take out more national debt  so lebanon resorted to running a ponzi  scheme they decided to pay their high  interest rates with money from new  depositors and this more or less sealed  their fate as people started to realize  what was happening many of them  attempted to withdraw their deposits  lebanon obviously could not sustain  these withdrawals so with no choice left  in late 2019 they decided to essentially  halt it withdrawals foreign currency  withdrawals were limited to just 200 to  300 per week and this would quickly turn  into zero dollars per week as soon as  the pandemic started technically the  government has yet to give up the peg  with the us dollar so according to  google the conversion rate is still the  same but the real conversion rate is  nowhere near this at this point you have  to use the black markets to make the  conversion and the black market rate  currently stands at 30 000 liras per  dollar this means that the purchasing  power of the lira has basically  collapsed 20x over the past three years  to make things worse corrupt government  officials are trying to run away with  the remaining bank reserves take the  central bank governor ryan solomay for  example he has allegedly transferred 400  million to foreign accounts for himself  and his relatives and that's just one  government official before the crisis  started lebanon's foreign currency  deposits stood at 176 billion dollars  but by july of 2020 this number  decreased to 114 billion  given that legitimate withdrawals were  essentially suspended during this time  period no one knows where exactly these  funds went but it's safe to assume that  they didn't make their way back to the  original depositors  just when you think things can't get  worse they did the entire world was hit  by the pandemic in early 2020 and  lebanon was by no means immune to this  in fact they were hit especially hard  given that tourism accounted for 18  of their pre-pandemic economy meanwhile  the government which was desperate for  revenue decided to implement a whatsapp  call tax the idea proposed charging six  dollars per month to use whatsapp for  calls as you would guess this made  people furious given that whatsapp was  their primary mode of communication  people would begin protesting outside  the government hq in beirut and the  government would cancel the tax within  hours but this was not enough to appease  the crowds while the whatsapp tax is  what sparked the protest there are  plenty of reasons to continue the  protest over the next few days tens of  thousands of lebanese would protest the  government's horrendous economic  policies from making the country overly  dependent on foreign capital to  leveraging a ponzi scheme and engaging  in blatant corruption there was no  question that much of the blame could be  placed directly on the government but  despite their best efforts these  protests didn't lead to anything that  substantial i mean even if the  government wanted to it's not like they  could just pass a new legislation that  would magically fix everything this  crisis had literally been brewing for 30  years so really the only straightforward  solution was to just start over from  ground zero all this economic and  political turmoil was already an  extremely challenging uphill battle but  things would get even worse you see on  august 4th 2020 beirut would experience  a deadly explosion 200 people were  killed 5 000 people were injured and 300  000 people were displaced at first there  was speculation that this might have  been a terrorist attack but this was  actually the result of ignorance and  mismanagement apparently  2750 tons of ammonium nitrate had been  unsafely stored at a warehouse to make  things worse multiple government  agencies had been warned about the  threat of this happening yet no action  was ever taken  at this point most lebanese have lost  all hope and they've been fleeing the  country in masses  mass emigration is nothing new for  lebanon however historic immigration was  never enough for the country's total  population to decline until 2015. since  then lebanon's population has decreased  from 6.4 million to 5.4 million and the  u.n expects that this decline will  continue for the next 10 years with all  of the most talented people leaving the  country it just becomes even less likely  that the country ever recovers as for  the people left in la banana government  has been trying to secure a bailout for  quite some time now but no one really  wants to bail out lebanon foreign  capital is one of the main reasons for  lebanon's current economic crisis so it  makes little sense to hand over even  more capital to the country not to  mention it the widespread corruption  across the government a few months ago  lebanon was able to secure a 3 billion  bailout from the international monetary  fund or the imf but 3 billion dollars is  nothing when it comes to running a 4  country and that lebanon is going to  need more money in no time despite this  dire economic situation many are still  hopeful that change is possible you see  it lebanon had a breakthrough election  in may 13 independent candidates from  the opposition were able to win seats in  parliament but given that their  parliament has 128 seats this is barely  10 percent so there's a ways to go in  terms of creating real political reform  but many lebanese are hopeful that this  is the first step in creating said  change in the meantime the lebanese will  have to continue putting up with the  rapidly devaluing currency  hyperinflation and the loads of  corruption not all with minimum foreign  help hopefully they'll make it to the  other side but my time will tell do you  think lebanon can recover comment that  down below  also drop a like if you're rooting for  the lebanese and of course consider  checking out our discord community to  suggest future video ideas and consider  subscribing and see more questions  logically answered but until then i'm  hari and i'll see you guys on the next  one

